1
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:10,620
Good evening, and welcome to The Quiet Archive.

2
00:00:10,620 --> 00:00:18,479
Tonight we spend a long November night inside an English castle, a little over five hundred years ago.

3
00:00:18,479 --> 00:00:21,483
There will be no sieges and no battles.

4
00:00:21,483 --> 00:00:32,962
We will simply arrive at dusk, pass through the gate, and move slowly inward, room by room, while the household lays down its work and goes to sleep.

5
00:00:34,712 --> 00:00:39,611
Make yourself comfortable, in whatever way your body likes best.

6
00:00:39,611 --> 00:00:43,056
Let your jaw loosen and your arms grow heavy.

7
00:00:43,056 --> 00:00:50,252
Breathe in slowly, and imagine cold autumn air with a trace of woodsmoke and wet leaves in it.

8
00:00:50,252 --> 00:00:51,859
Hold it for a moment.

9
00:00:51,859 --> 00:00:58,136
Then let it go, long and slow, and feel the bed take a little more of your weight.

10
00:00:59,886 --> 00:01:02,724
You do not need to follow every word.

11
00:01:02,724 --> 00:01:08,399
If your thoughts wander off, let them go, and come back whenever you like.

12
00:01:08,399 --> 00:01:20,516
The castle will keep its own quiet hours, and if you drift away before we reach the last room, the night will go on without you, gently, exactly as it should.

13
00:01:30,265 --> 00:01:38,366
We begin on a muddy lane between hedges, a mile or so from the castle, as the light goes out of the sky.

14
00:01:38,366 --> 00:01:49,893
The fields on either side have been ploughed and sown with winter wheat, and the furrows hold little strips of silver water from the afternoon rain.

15
00:01:49,893 --> 00:01:56,591
A few rooks are flying home in a loose, untidy line, calling to each other as they go.

16
00:01:58,341 --> 00:02:05,281
Ahead of you, on a low rise above a river, the castle stands against the last of the sunset.

17
00:02:05,281 --> 00:02:20,370
You can see the long grey curtain wall, with round towers set along it at intervals, and the tall gatehouse in the middle, with two towers of its own pressed close together on either side of the arch.

18
00:02:20,370 --> 00:02:25,349
Behind the wall rises the old square keep, the oldest part of all.

19
00:02:27,099 --> 00:02:38,069
The keep was built long ago, perhaps three or four hundred years before tonight, when the first lords came here and wanted one strong tower to live in.

20
00:02:38,069 --> 00:02:55,579
Over the centuries the castle grew around it, a ring of wall, then towers, then a great hall, then kitchens and chambers and a chapel, until the old tower became only one building among many, like a grandfather sitting quietly in the corner.

21
00:02:57,329 --> 00:03:06,963
You walk on, past a mill where the wheel has been stopped for the night and the water slides over the weir with a long, steady hiss.

22
00:03:06,963 --> 00:03:09,809
A miller's lad is closing the shutters.

23
00:03:09,809 --> 00:03:14,189
A dog barks once from the yard and then thinks better of it.

24
00:03:14,189 --> 00:03:21,414
The smell of the river rises up, cold and green, with a little mud and a little rotting leaf in it.

25
00:03:23,164 --> 00:03:35,588
As you come closer, you can make out the narrow windows in the towers, most of them dark, a few showing a faint glow where a candle or a fire is burning in a chamber.

26
00:03:35,588 --> 00:03:50,855
Thin threads of smoke rise from several chimneys, and from the louvre on the roof of the great hall, and lean slowly eastward in the breeze, until you cannot tell where the smoke ends and the dusk begins.

27
00:03:52,605 --> 00:04:00,844
The lane turns, and now you can see the moat, a broad ribbon of still water lying around the foot of the walls.

28
00:04:00,844 --> 00:04:03,441
It is not a menacing thing tonight.

29
00:04:03,441 --> 00:04:19,399
Ducks have settled at its edge among the reeds, heads tucked under their wings, and the reflections of the towers lie upside down in it, perfectly still, with here and there a yellow window trembling on the surface.

30
00:04:21,149 --> 00:04:28,616
High on the gatehouse a flag is stirring in the evening breeze, slowly unrolling and folding again.

31
00:04:28,616 --> 00:04:36,762
It carries the colours of the family who hold this castle, and when they are here, the flag flies to say so.

32
00:04:36,762 --> 00:04:38,346
Tonight it is flying.

33
00:04:38,346 --> 00:04:49,886
The lord and his household have come to stay for the winter, with their carts of bedding and chests and hangings, as they do every year after Michaelmas.

34
00:04:51,635 --> 00:05:02,673
A great household was always on the move in those days, travelling from one house to another through the year, eating the stores of each place in turn.

35
00:05:02,673 --> 00:05:06,474
When they arrived, the empty rooms came alive again.

36
00:05:06,474 --> 00:05:18,899
Beds were set up, painted cloths were hung on the walls, the chapel was furnished with its silver and its candles, and the kitchens were lit for the first time in months.

37
00:05:20,649 --> 00:05:27,812
Along the road behind you, a few late travellers are hurrying to reach the gate before it closes.

38
00:05:27,812 --> 00:05:32,390
A carter with a load of firewood, walking at his horse's head.

39
00:05:32,390 --> 00:05:36,673
Two women with baskets of eggs and cheese for the kitchen.

40
00:05:36,673 --> 00:05:46,420
A boy with a bundle of rushes on his back, bent almost double, cut from the riverbank that morning to freshen the floor of the hall.

41
00:05:56,170 --> 00:06:06,116
You cross the wooden bridge over the moat, your footsteps sounding hollow on the planks, and pass under the deep arch of the gatehouse.

42
00:06:06,116 --> 00:06:09,653
It is like walking through a short stone tunnel.

43
00:06:09,653 --> 00:06:21,514
Above you, in the vaulted roof, there are narrow slots and openings, and set into the walls on either side are the grooves where the portcullis runs up and down.

44
00:06:23,264 --> 00:06:33,929
The porter is waiting in his little lodge just inside the arch, a small room with a fire, a stool, and a window looking out onto the passage.

45
00:06:33,929 --> 00:06:42,703
He is an old man with a grey beard and a heavy ring of keys at his belt, and he has kept this gate for twenty years.

46
00:06:42,703 --> 00:06:49,888
He knows every carter and every kitchen girl by name, and he nods to each of them as they pass.

47
00:06:51,638 --> 00:07:01,916
When the last of the travellers is through, he steps out into the passage, looks once along the empty road, and calls up to the room above.

48
00:07:01,916 --> 00:07:08,348
There, two men take hold of the spokes of a great wooden windlass and begin to turn it.

49
00:07:08,348 --> 00:07:16,555
With a slow creaking of rope and timber, the end of the drawbridge lifts from the bank and rises into the dark.

50
00:07:18,305 --> 00:07:20,683
Then the portcullis comes down.

51
00:07:20,683 --> 00:07:33,566
It is a heavy grid of oak, bound and pointed with iron, and it slides down its stone grooves with a long, low rumble, until it settles onto the ground with a soft thud.

52
00:07:33,566 --> 00:07:39,164
The great wooden doors are swung closed behind it and barred with a beam.

53
00:07:39,164 --> 00:07:44,225
The castle is shut for the night, and everyone inside can feel it.

54
00:07:45,975 --> 00:07:53,618
The porter locks the small wicket door, cut into one of the larger ones, and tests it with his hand.

55
00:07:53,618 --> 00:08:03,630
Then he takes the keys and carries them across the courtyard to the constable, the officer who keeps the castle in the lord's name.

56
00:08:03,630 --> 00:08:10,509
They will stay in the constable's chamber until morning, hanging on a nail beside his bed.

57
00:08:12,259 --> 00:08:22,446
Now you are standing in the outer courtyard, the bailey, a wide space of beaten earth and cobbles, enclosed on every side by the walls.

58
00:08:22,446 --> 00:08:25,917
In daylight it is as busy as a village street.

59
00:08:25,917 --> 00:08:40,632
There are stables and storehouses built against the inside of the wall, a smithy with its anvil, a brewhouse, a pen of geese, and a well in the middle, with a little stone roof over its windlass.

60
00:08:42,382 --> 00:08:58,262
Across the courtyard, a groom is leading the carter's horse to the stables, and the firewood is being stacked under a lean-to roof against the wall, log upon log, enough for a week of fires in the hall and the kitchens.

61
00:08:58,262 --> 00:09:09,283
The carter himself will sleep tonight in the hayloft above the horses, where the air is warm and smells of hay, and he will set off home at first light.

62
00:09:11,033 --> 00:09:19,922
The smith has let his forge die down, and only a dull red eye of coal still shows in the hearth behind his half-open door.

63
00:09:19,922 --> 00:09:32,600
In the stables, the horses are pulling hay from their racks, and you can hear the steady grinding of their teeth, and every so often the soft blow of a breath through a nose.

64
00:09:32,600 --> 00:09:37,700
One of them puts its head out over the half-door and watches you pass.

65
00:09:39,450 --> 00:09:51,201
Up on the wall, a dovecote is built into the corner of a tower, and the pigeons are settling for the night, murmuring to each other in their little stone holes.

66
00:09:51,201 --> 00:09:57,957
A cat is sitting on the edge of the well, perfectly still, looking at nothing in particular.

67
00:09:57,957 --> 00:10:05,669
And from somewhere near the kitchens comes a smell that makes the whole courtyard feel warmer: new bread.

68
00:10:15,419 --> 00:10:26,930
The bakehouse is a low stone building with a tall chimney, close against the curtain wall, well away from the timber stables in case of sparks.

69
00:10:26,930 --> 00:10:34,739
Its door stands half open, and inside, the great domed oven glows like the mouth of a small cave.

70
00:10:34,739 --> 00:10:42,064
The baker is a broad, floury man in a linen apron, and his work is nearly done for the day.

71
00:10:43,814 --> 00:10:53,782
An oven like this is heated by burning bundles of brushwood right inside it, until the stone of the dome is hot all the way through.

72
00:10:53,782 --> 00:11:03,826
Then the ashes are raked out, the floor is swept with a damp mop of rags on a pole, and the loaves are slid in on a long wooden peel.

73
00:11:03,826 --> 00:11:09,414
The heat stored in the stone does the baking, slowly falling hour by hour.

74
00:11:11,164 --> 00:11:13,892
Tonight the last batch is coming out.

75
00:11:13,892 --> 00:11:19,347
The fine white loaves, made with the best flour, are for the lord's table.

76
00:11:19,347 --> 00:11:24,212
The darker loaves, of wheat mixed with rye, are for the household.

77
00:11:24,212 --> 00:11:37,703
And there are the trenchers, thick slices of coarse, stale bread, used as plates at dinner, which soak up the juices of the meat and are afterwards given away to the poor at the gate.

78
00:11:39,453 --> 00:11:45,852
The baker's boy lifts the loaves out one by one and sets them on a long board to cool.

79
00:11:45,852 --> 00:11:48,976
They crackle softly as the crusts tighten.

80
00:11:48,976 --> 00:12:03,560
When the board is full, he covers them with a cloth, and the warm, sweet, yeasty smell drifts out through the door and across the cold courtyard, mixing with the woodsmoke and the smell of horses.

81
00:12:05,310 --> 00:12:08,139
Then the baker begins tomorrow's bread.

82
00:12:08,139 --> 00:12:18,369
In a long wooden trough he mixes flour and water with a lump of yesterday's dough, saved for this very purpose, which holds the living yeast.

83
00:12:18,369 --> 00:12:26,496
He works it with his fists and his forearms, folding and pressing, for a long time, until it is smooth and soft.

84
00:12:26,496 --> 00:12:33,533
Then he covers the trough with a cloth and leaves it to rise in the warmth of the oven all night.

85
00:12:35,284 --> 00:12:40,237
Outside, a kitchen girl comes to the well with two wooden buckets.

86
00:12:40,237 --> 00:12:52,022
She hooks the first one to the rope and lets it down, and the windlass turns by itself, rattling, faster and faster, until there is a faint splash far below.

87
00:12:52,022 --> 00:12:59,753
The well is very deep, cut down through the rock so that the castle would always have water of its own.

88
00:12:59,753 --> 00:13:03,806
She winds the bucket up again, slowly, hand over hand.

89
00:13:05,556 --> 00:13:09,845
The water is so cold it seems to smoke in the evening air.

90
00:13:09,845 --> 00:13:19,161
She fills her second bucket, sets the yoke across her shoulders, and walks carefully back to the kitchen, not spilling a drop.

91
00:13:19,161 --> 00:13:30,474
The cat follows her a little way, hoping for something, and then gives up and sits down again in the doorway of the bakehouse, where the stones are warm.

92
00:13:32,224 --> 00:13:35,835
Across the courtyard, the kitchen is closing too.

93
00:13:35,835 --> 00:13:40,773
The great pots have been scoured with sand and hung on their hooks.

94
00:13:40,773 --> 00:13:44,458
The spits have been wiped and laid on their racks.

95
00:13:44,458 --> 00:13:58,314
The cook, a small, brisk woman who has ruled this place since before dawn, sits for a moment on a stool by the dying fire, eats a heel of bread with a piece of cheese, and closes her eyes.

96
00:14:00,064 --> 00:14:09,055
High above the courtyard, the sky has turned a deep, clear blue, with the first stars coming out over the battlements.

97
00:14:09,055 --> 00:14:12,331
A thin moon is rising beyond the gatehouse.

98
00:14:12,331 --> 00:14:21,702
From the great hall, across the courtyard, comes the murmur of many voices, the scrape of benches, and a burst of laughter.

99
00:14:21,702 --> 00:14:22,998
Supper is ending.

100
00:14:22,998 --> 00:14:26,198
Let us go in and find a place by the fire.

101
00:14:35,948 --> 00:14:45,667
You come in through a small doorway and find yourself in a narrow passage, with a wooden screen on one side, pierced by two openings.

102
00:14:45,667 --> 00:14:47,713
This is the screens passage.

103
00:14:47,713 --> 00:14:59,114
On your other side are three doors, one to the kitchen, one to the buttery, where the ale and wine are kept, and one to the pantry, where the bread is kept.

104
00:14:59,114 --> 00:15:03,937
Servants are hurrying in and out of all of them with empty dishes.

105
00:15:05,687 --> 00:15:10,870
Step through the screen, and the great hall opens up in front of you.

106
00:15:10,870 --> 00:15:17,707
It is long and high, like a barn made of stone, and it is the heart of the whole household.

107
00:15:17,707 --> 00:15:28,900
Far above, in the shadows, the roof is held up by great beams of oak, with curved braces springing from the walls, like the ribs of an upturned ship.

108
00:15:28,900 --> 00:15:31,906
Firelight moves softly across all of it.

109
00:15:33,656 --> 00:15:40,567
In the very middle of the floor, on a raised square of stones and tiles, the fire is burning.

110
00:15:40,567 --> 00:15:42,054
There is no chimney.

111
00:15:42,054 --> 00:15:55,134
The smoke rises straight up into the roof and finds its way out through a louvre, a little turret of wooden slats on the ridge, which lets the smoke out and keeps the rain off.

112
00:15:55,134 --> 00:15:58,850
The air up there is always a little blue and hazy.

113
00:16:00,600 --> 00:16:11,307
At the far end of the hall, on a low wooden platform called the dais, stands the high table, where the lord and lady have eaten with their guests.

114
00:16:11,307 --> 00:16:20,988
Behind it, on the wall, hangs a great painted cloth, with a hunting scene of hounds and a white hart running through a green forest.

115
00:16:20,988 --> 00:16:27,075
Tonight the lord and lady have already risen and gone up to their own rooms beyond.

116
00:16:28,825 --> 00:16:33,353
Down in the body of the hall, the household is clearing away.

117
00:16:33,353 --> 00:16:35,579
The tables here are not fixed.

118
00:16:35,579 --> 00:16:49,460
Each is only a set of long wooden boards laid across trestles, and now the boards are being lifted off, one by one, and stacked against the wall, and the trestles folded and carried away.

119
00:16:49,460 --> 00:16:55,769
In a few minutes the whole long room is clear, and the floor is open from end to end.

120
00:16:57,519 --> 00:17:06,496
The floor is covered with rushes from the river, spread thick, and mixed with sweet-smelling herbs to keep the air fresh.

121
00:17:06,496 --> 00:17:16,882
Tonight the boy you saw on the road is scattering his new bundle over the old, and the hall fills with the green, grassy smell of cut stems.

122
00:17:16,882 --> 00:17:24,079
When he treads on a sprig of dried lavender, its scent rises around him for a moment and is gone.

123
00:17:25,829 --> 00:17:32,526
A great household might have many dozens of people in it, sometimes more than a hundred.

124
00:17:32,526 --> 00:17:40,821
There were officers and clerks, cooks and scullions, grooms and carters, laundresses and pages and chaplains.

125
00:17:40,821 --> 00:17:52,922
The great hall was where most of them ate, and where many of them gathered in the evenings, to talk, to mend things, to play at dice, and to listen to a story.

126
00:17:54,672 --> 00:18:05,369
At this hour, a minstrel is sitting on a bench near the fire with a small harp on his knee, playing something slow and quiet, almost to himself.

127
00:18:05,369 --> 00:18:10,494
A few people are listening, their faces lit warm and gold from below.

128
00:18:10,494 --> 00:18:13,317
An old man is mending a leather strap.

129
00:18:13,317 --> 00:18:21,488
Two pages, no more than ten or eleven years old, are already yawning, leaning against each other on the bench.

130
00:18:23,238 --> 00:18:33,444
Near the hearth, a big grey hound has stretched out on its side in the warmth, its legs twitching a little as it dreams of running.

131
00:18:33,444 --> 00:18:41,702
Another lies with its long nose on its paws, watching the fire, its eyes slowly closing and opening again.

132
00:18:41,702 --> 00:18:51,206
Every so often a log settles in the flames with a soft crunch, and a little drift of sparks rises up toward the dark roof.

133
00:19:00,956 --> 00:19:05,162
From across the courtyard, a small bell begins to ring.

134
00:19:05,162 --> 00:19:11,738
It is the chapel bell, calling the household to compline, the last service of the day.

135
00:19:11,738 --> 00:19:17,855
A few people rise from the benches and wrap their cloaks around them and go out.

136
00:19:17,855 --> 00:19:26,343
In the little chapel, lit by beeswax candles, the chaplain and his clerks are already standing in their places.

137
00:19:28,093 --> 00:19:30,253
Compline is short and simple.

138
00:19:30,253 --> 00:19:39,860
It is sung softly, in Latin, in the half dark, and its words ask for peace through the night, and for protection from bad dreams.

139
00:19:39,860 --> 00:19:48,202
One of the hymns sung at this hour begins with the words Te lucis ante terminum, before the ending of the light.

140
00:19:48,202 --> 00:19:57,512
Then the candles are snuffed, one by one, and the chapel is left to the small red lamp that burns before the altar all night.

141
00:19:59,262 --> 00:20:02,980
Back in the hall, the evening is coming to an end.

142
00:20:02,980 --> 00:20:12,424
One of the servants brings a great domed cover and sets it down over the embers of the fire, leaving only a little gap for air.

143
00:20:12,424 --> 00:20:25,288
The fire will smoulder slowly under it all night, safe from sparks, and in the morning it can be uncovered and blown back into flame, without needing to be lit from nothing.

144
00:20:27,038 --> 00:20:30,226
This is the old custom of covering the fire.

145
00:20:30,226 --> 00:20:35,299
In French it was called couvre-feu, and from it comes our word curfew.

146
00:20:35,299 --> 00:20:45,950
In many towns a bell was rung at a fixed hour each evening, as a signal that fires should be covered or put out, and people should go home to rest.

147
00:20:45,950 --> 00:20:56,457
The story goes that William the Conqueror brought the custom to England, though more likely it was already here, and he only made people keep it.

148
00:20:58,207 --> 00:21:02,571
Then the beds are made, right here on the floor of the hall.

149
00:21:02,571 --> 00:21:14,936
The lower servants, the grooms and the kitchen boys and the porters' lads, unroll their straw pallets and blankets along the walls and near the warm stones of the hearth.

150
00:21:14,936 --> 00:21:17,773
Some have a sack of chaff for a pillow.

151
00:21:17,773 --> 00:21:20,246
Others simply roll up their cloak.

152
00:21:20,246 --> 00:21:25,556
The hall is the warmest room in the castle, and nobody minds the company.

153
00:21:27,306 --> 00:21:42,188
Each sleeper is given his small allowance for the night, which the household books call livery: perhaps a portion of bread and a measure of ale, so that no one should wake hungry or thirsty in the dark.

154
00:21:42,188 --> 00:21:52,650
You can see them settling down, taking a last mouthful, pulling their blankets up to their chins, turning onto their sides, and growing still.

155
00:21:54,400 --> 00:22:07,826
In the chambers above the hall, the gentlemen and officers of the household are going to bed too, two or three to a room, and often two to a bed, which was thought perfectly ordinary.

156
00:22:07,826 --> 00:22:20,299
They hang their belts on pegs, fold their gowns over a wooden pole, and climb in under the blankets, talking quietly for a few minutes, until one of them stops answering.

157
00:22:22,049 --> 00:22:30,059
The minstrel has put away his harp in its bag and lain down on a bench, with his hood pulled over his eyes.

158
00:22:30,059 --> 00:22:35,599
The two pages have been taken off to their own corner by an older servant.

159
00:22:35,599 --> 00:22:41,438
Someone pulls the curtain across the screens passage, to keep out the draught.

160
00:22:41,438 --> 00:22:50,721
The rushlights are put out, one after another, until the only light is the faint red glow seeping from under the fire cover.

161
00:22:52,471 --> 00:22:59,797
In the dimness, the great roof seems to grow even higher, the beams fading up into darkness.

162
00:22:59,797 --> 00:23:07,441
The smoke from the covered fire rises thin and slow, a pale thread winding up toward the louvre.

163
00:23:07,441 --> 00:23:10,148
Someone is already snoring softly.

164
00:23:10,148 --> 00:23:12,139
A hound sighs and shifts.

165
00:23:12,139 --> 00:23:14,050
A log ticks as it cools.

166
00:23:14,050 --> 00:23:21,058
And the long hall, which was so full of noise an hour ago, becomes as quiet as a church.

167
00:23:30,808 --> 00:23:43,027
Let us climb now, up a narrow spiral stair in the thickness of a tower wall, round and round in the dark, with one hand on the cold, worn stone of the central pillar.

168
00:23:43,027 --> 00:23:47,296
The steps are hollowed in the middle by centuries of feet.

169
00:23:47,296 --> 00:23:53,405
Here and there a slit window lets in a blade of moonlight and a breath of cold air.

170
00:23:53,405 --> 00:23:58,264
And then a little door, and you step out onto the top of the wall.

171
00:24:00,014 --> 00:24:10,642
You are standing on the wall walk, a stone path running along the top of the curtain wall, from tower to tower, all the way around the castle.

172
00:24:10,642 --> 00:24:21,495
On the outer side rise the battlements, a row of solid stone teeth with gaps between them, so that a watcher can look out and still have shelter.

173
00:24:21,495 --> 00:24:26,211
The stone is wet with dew and shining faintly in the moonlight.

174
00:24:27,961 --> 00:24:37,860
In the corner of a tower, a little iron brazier is glowing, and beside it, wrapped in a thick woollen cloak, stands the night watchman.

175
00:24:37,860 --> 00:24:42,113
In a great household of this time he was called the wayte.

176
00:24:42,113 --> 00:24:53,113
He has a pipe hanging on a cord around his neck, a lantern at his feet, and a long staff in his hand, and he has been awake since the gate was closed.

177
00:24:54,863 --> 00:25:03,295
His duties were written down in the household books of the time, which set out how the king's own court should be run.

178
00:25:03,295 --> 00:25:16,800
On winter nights, they say, from Michaelmas until Holy Thursday before Easter, the wayte should pipe the watch four times through the night, and three times on the shorter nights of summer.

179
00:25:16,800 --> 00:25:21,231
In this castle, the steward has borrowed the same good custom.

180
00:25:22,981 --> 00:25:34,102
The books also say he must keep a careful watch at every chamber door and every office, against fire, and against thieves, and against any other danger.

181
00:25:34,102 --> 00:25:49,541
So every so often he takes up his lantern and walks the length of the wall, and then goes down into the courtyard and makes his round, listening at doors, checking that the fires are covered and the candles out.

182
00:25:51,291 --> 00:26:05,863
And for this he was given his reward: he ate in the hall with the minstrels, and at night he was allowed his livery, half a loaf and half a gallon of ale, to keep him going through the long hours.

183
00:26:05,863 --> 00:26:11,959
On summer nights the list adds a couple of candles, and some coal for his brazier.

184
00:26:11,959 --> 00:26:15,974
It was not a grand position, but it was a trusted one.

185
00:26:17,723 --> 00:26:30,133
Now he lifts the pipe to his lips and plays a few slow notes, clear and high, which float out over the sleeping courtyard and the dark roofs and away over the moat.

186
00:26:30,133 --> 00:26:31,570
It is not an alarm.

187
00:26:31,570 --> 00:26:33,083
It is a reassurance.

188
00:26:33,083 --> 00:26:40,272
It says that the hour has come round, that someone is awake and watching, and that all is well.

189
00:26:40,272 --> 00:26:43,601
Then he lowers the pipe and is silent again.

190
00:26:45,351 --> 00:26:50,925
From up here you can see the whole countryside, silver and grey under the moon.

191
00:26:50,925 --> 00:26:56,146
The river curves away between its willows, with a little mist lying on it.

192
00:26:56,146 --> 00:27:12,444
Beyond the river there is a village, a cluster of dark thatched roofs around a church with a square stone tower, and one tiny window still lit, where perhaps someone is sitting up with a sick child, or a cow is calving in the barn.

193
00:27:14,194 --> 00:27:19,158
Far away, on the edge of the sky, the low line of a wooded hill.

194
00:27:19,158 --> 00:27:26,992
Nearer, the strips of the open fields, the dark shapes of haystacks, a windmill with its sails still.

195
00:27:26,992 --> 00:27:35,290
An owl is calling from the woods, a long, wavering note, and a second owl answers it from much farther off.

196
00:27:35,290 --> 00:27:42,503
The stars are very bright and very many, and they turn slowly, all together, over the towers.

197
00:27:44,253 --> 00:28:08,104
The watchman pulls his cloak tighter, warms his hands at the brazier for a moment, and then sets off along the wall walk with his lantern swinging, a small gold light moving steadily away from you between the dark stone teeth of the battlements, until it reaches the next tower and turns the corner and is gone.

198
00:28:17,854 --> 00:28:29,078
Now we go inward one last time, beyond the dais at the end of the great hall, through a door and up a short stair, to the private rooms of the family.

199
00:28:29,078 --> 00:28:42,921
Here the walls are plastered and painted, a soft red with a pattern of little white flowers, and the floors are of wooden boards, laid with rush matting, warm and quiet under your feet.

200
00:28:44,671 --> 00:28:46,749
This is the lady's chamber.

201
00:28:46,749 --> 00:28:51,906
It is not large, but it is the most comfortable room in the castle.

202
00:28:51,906 --> 00:29:02,913
A small fire is burning in a hooded fireplace set into the wall, with a proper chimney of its own, so there is no smoke in the air here at all.

203
00:29:02,913 --> 00:29:11,764
It throws a soft, moving light across the floor, and the room smells faintly of woodsmoke and of dried rose petals.

204
00:29:13,514 --> 00:29:16,570
Against the far wall stands the great bed.

205
00:29:16,570 --> 00:29:25,809
It is the most precious piece of furniture in the whole house, worth more than all the benches and tables in the hall together.

206
00:29:25,809 --> 00:29:40,650
Above it hangs a canopy, called the tester, and from the canopy fall heavy curtains of dyed wool, blue with a border of embroidered vines, which can be drawn right around the bed to shut out the draughts.

207
00:29:42,400 --> 00:29:45,571
The bed is built up in layers, like a cake.

208
00:29:45,571 --> 00:29:48,888
At the bottom, a mattress stuffed with straw.

209
00:29:48,888 --> 00:29:52,796
On top of that, a deep mattress filled with feathers.

210
00:29:52,796 --> 00:30:01,422
Then linen sheets, very fine and very white, which were washed and dried on the hedges and folded away with lavender.

211
00:30:01,422 --> 00:30:03,045
Then woollen blankets.

212
00:30:03,045 --> 00:30:08,427
And over everything, a coverlet lined with soft grey fur, heavy and warm.

213
00:30:10,177 --> 00:30:19,447
At the foot of the bed stands a carved wooden chest, which holds the lady's gowns and veils, and which also serves as a seat.

214
00:30:19,447 --> 00:30:30,350
Low down beside the great bed, there is a little truckle bed on wheels, which can be pulled out at night and pushed back underneath in the morning.

215
00:30:30,350 --> 00:30:35,393
Her maid sleeps there, close by, in case she is needed in the night.

216
00:30:37,143 --> 00:30:53,781
In the thickness of the wall there is a window, set back in a deep embrasure, with a stone bench built into each side, facing each other, so that two people can sit there and look out, or sew in the daylight, or talk quietly.

217
00:30:53,781 --> 00:31:02,359
Tonight the wooden shutters are half closed, and a long bar of moonlight falls through the gap and across the floor.

218
00:31:04,109 --> 00:31:11,774
In a little niche in the wall, cut into the stone for just this purpose, a single candle is burning.

219
00:31:11,774 --> 00:31:19,516
It is made of beeswax, not tallow, so it burns with a clear, steady flame and a faint smell of honey.

220
00:31:19,516 --> 00:31:28,407
Wax candles like this were costly, and in this room they are a small luxury, the last light of the day before sleep.

221
00:31:30,157 --> 00:31:38,685
On a cushion by the fire, a little white dog is curled up in a perfect circle, its nose tucked under its tail.

222
00:31:38,685 --> 00:31:48,919
Lap dogs like this were loved and spoiled in great households, and sometimes appear carved at the feet of their mistresses on tombs.

223
00:31:48,919 --> 00:31:57,059
It opens one eye as the fire settles, looks around the room, finds that all is well, and closes it again.

224
00:31:58,809 --> 00:32:12,757
On the wall beside the bed hangs a woven cloth, showing a garden enclosed by a low wall, with a fountain in the middle, and trees heavy with fruit, and little birds among the flowers.

225
00:32:12,757 --> 00:32:22,894
When the draught from the window touches it, it sways very slightly on its rail, as if the garden itself were breathing in its sleep.

226
00:32:24,644 --> 00:32:37,151
The lady herself is sitting on the edge of the bed while her maid unpins her veil and brushes out her long hair, slowly, stroke after stroke, the way it is done every night.

227
00:32:37,151 --> 00:32:45,755
They talk quietly about small things: the journey here, the cold in the chapel, a letter that must be written tomorrow.

228
00:32:45,755 --> 00:32:53,273
Then the veil is folded away in the chest, the lady lies down, and the curtains are drawn almost closed.

229
00:33:03,023 --> 00:33:05,154
Now it is your turn to rest.

230
00:33:05,154 --> 00:33:16,337
Imagine that the bed curtains have been drawn almost closed around you, leaving just a narrow gap where the firelight comes through, soft and gold.

231
00:33:16,337 --> 00:33:19,533
The feather mattress has taken your shape.

232
00:33:19,533 --> 00:33:22,196
The fur coverlet is heavy and warm.

233
00:33:22,196 --> 00:33:25,999
There is nothing left that you need to do tonight.

234
00:33:27,749 --> 00:33:32,550
Far below, the great hall is dark and full of slow breathing.

235
00:33:32,550 --> 00:33:37,587
The bread is rising quietly in its trough in the warm bakehouse.

236
00:33:37,587 --> 00:33:41,600
The horses are dozing on their feet in the stables.

237
00:33:41,600 --> 00:33:47,975
The drawbridge is up, the gate is barred, and the keys are hanging on their nail.

238
00:33:47,975 --> 00:33:51,280
Everything is closed, and safe, and still.

239
00:33:53,030 --> 00:33:59,906
Let your breathing slow until it matches the deep, even breathing of the sleepers in the hall.

240
00:33:59,906 --> 00:34:06,051
Let your thoughts grow quiet, the way the hall grew quiet when the fire was covered.

241
00:34:06,051 --> 00:34:16,731
And if a thought does come, let it rise and drift away like the thin thread of smoke going up into the louvre, and out into the cold, clear night.

242
00:34:18,481 --> 00:34:27,985
And somewhere up on the walls, the watchman is walking his round under the stars, his lantern moving slowly from tower to tower.

243
00:34:27,985 --> 00:34:40,162
In a little while he will lift his pipe again, and a few soft notes will drift over the roofs, to tell the sleeping castle that the hour has turned and all is well.

244
00:34:40,162 --> 00:34:43,057
You will not need to wake to hear them.

245
00:34:44,807 --> 00:34:51,133
Thank you for spending this November night in the castle with The Quiet Archive.

246
00:34:51,133 --> 00:34:59,830
The candle is burning low in its niche, the fire is settling into embers, and the moon is shining on the moat.

247
00:34:59,830 --> 00:35:00,700
Sleep well.

248
00:35:00,700 --> 00:35:01,490
Goodnight.
